1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Forget about the rod and reel.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:08,000
Commercial fishermen mean business.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Often they bet their lives on this high-stakes game.

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:17,000
With the latest high-tech electronics and strong and sophisticated nets,

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
landing the catch is an exercise of deadly efficiency.

6
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,000
But are these boats cleaning out the seas?

7
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Is there a better way?

8
00:00:47,029 --> 00:00:49,240
Now, commercial fishing on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:49,241 --> 00:00:55,820
In Alaska's Bering Sea, monstrous ships stalk schools of fish with precision and mechanical

10
00:00:55,821 --> 00:01:02,820
muscle.

11
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:11,140
At 376 feet, the Alaska Ocean is one of the largest commercial fishing vessels on Earth.

12
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:17,860
But in the North Pacific, size takes a back seat to high technology.

13
00:01:17,861 --> 00:01:19,260
The ship's bridge

14
00:01:19,540 --> 00:01:25,180
contains the latest in navigational gear and a series of monitors that display the cold,

15
00:01:25,181 --> 00:01:28,880
calculated hunt for fish.

16
00:01:28,881 --> 00:01:34,400
Sometimes you can get a hundred tons of fish inside of five or ten minutes.

17
00:01:34,401 --> 00:01:39,640
Every piece of electronics we've come to rely heavily on and you hate to see any

18
00:01:39,641 --> 00:01:41,440
of them go out.

19
00:01:41,441 --> 00:01:47,900
It's called fish finding technology and it's revolutionized commercial fishing.

20
00:01:47,901 --> 00:01:52,420
The main advantages are if you can get out and find a fish, you're saving fuel,

21
00:01:52,421 --> 00:01:54,800
you're saving time, you're saving wear and tear on your boat.

22
00:01:56,820 --> 00:02:00,160
It was World War II that opened the door on finding fish.

23
00:02:01,740 --> 00:02:04,300
The sonar developed to hunt enemy submarines

24
00:02:04,301 --> 00:02:07,240
is now making fishermen more efficient,

25
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:10,980
a key to survival in a heavily regulated business.

26
00:02:12,620 --> 00:02:14,720
Thousands of miles from Alaskan waters,

27
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:17,700
the White Dove II fishes for tuna

28
00:02:17,701 --> 00:02:20,100
out of the historic port of Gloucester in Massachusetts.

29
00:02:22,420 --> 00:02:28,600
The regulations that are required for fishing these days range everywhere from how many days you can go fishing,

30
00:02:29,240 --> 00:02:33,680
how long you can stay out, how many fish you can have on board of a particular species, etc., etc.

31
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:37,840
And with many of these guys down to 50 days a year, you have to be very efficient,

32
00:02:38,140 --> 00:02:42,000
get on the fish, find the fish, turn around and come home and minimize your days used at sea.

33
00:02:42,001 --> 00:02:52,540
Small fishing boats also depend on the latest in fish-finding gear.

34
00:02:54,760 --> 00:03:03,500
A fish-finder or acoustic echo-sounder transmits a sound echo or ping from a transducer straight down to the ocean floor.

35
00:03:04,340 --> 00:03:05,563
So your pulse goes down.

36
00:03:05,564 --> 00:03:09,180
If it hits a fish, generally what it's transmitting off is the bladder,

37
00:03:09,181 --> 00:03:12,280
the air bladder inside the fish, which will vary between species.

38
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:15,160
Certain fish will look stronger at certain different frequencies,

39
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:19,440
and they'll use those different frequencies to help differentiate between species and the like.

40
00:03:20,360 --> 00:03:25,300
A more sophisticated omni-sonar uses multiple transducers

41
00:03:25,301 --> 00:03:28,000
that ping 360 degrees around the vessel.

42
00:03:28,980 --> 00:03:30,940
What that allows you to do is see the targets.

43
00:03:31,300 --> 00:03:33,300
Depending on the frequency and the range that you've selected,

44
00:03:33,380 --> 00:03:35,280
that allows you to see the targets around the vessel,

45
00:03:35,281 --> 00:03:37,520
as opposed to the echo-sonder, which shows them under the vessel.

46
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:41,940
But pinpointing a school of fish is only half the picture.

47
00:03:43,340 --> 00:03:46,560
The computerized chart plotter has replaced paper nautical charts

48
00:03:46,561 --> 00:03:50,500
with a system capable of using global positioning

49
00:03:50,501 --> 00:03:54,580
to map, monitor and record the ocean bottom in 3D.

50
00:03:55,540 --> 00:03:57,360
So now by just glancing over at the chart,

51
00:03:57,780 --> 00:04:00,460
you can see yourself positioned on the bottom.

52
00:04:00,461 --> 00:04:04,680
So you can record where you've been, you can put marks where certain features are.

53
00:04:04,780 --> 00:04:08,620
Maybe there's a wreck there which tends to collect fish or damaged nets,

54
00:04:08,621 --> 00:04:09,780
that you may want to avoid.

55
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:15,800
Today, fishing on a tight schedule with pinpoint accuracy

56
00:04:15,801 --> 00:04:19,520
is mandatory for a small commercial fisherman to stay in business.

57
00:04:20,660 --> 00:04:24,160
But this centuries-old tradition may have endangered itself.

58
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:28,400
Overfishing or the severe depletion of certain fish species

59
00:04:28,401 --> 00:04:31,080
threatens both man and nature.

60
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:36,940
According to the UN's Food and Agriculture Organization,

61
00:04:36,941 --> 00:04:45,180
about 60% of all commercially important fish types are fully exploited, overexploited, or depleted.

62
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:50,720
But the fishing continues while one reality doesn't change.

63
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:56,180
Commercial fishing remains one of the most dangerous occupations in the world.

64
00:04:56,181 --> 00:05:08,080
Working on a constantly moving platform with the threat of rogue waves and sudden devastating fires,

65
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:12,900
fishermen face potential disaster every day they set out to sea.

66
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:20,540
The evolution of fishing gear in North America begins with the fish weir.

67
00:05:20,541 --> 00:05:28,320
In 1913, during the excavation of the Boston subway, workers unearthed a Native American

68
00:05:28,321 --> 00:05:31,900
fish weir dated at nearly 4,000 years old.

69
00:05:32,740 --> 00:05:38,660
The weir resembled a fence with an estimated 60,000 stakes used to trap fish during low

70
00:05:38,661 --> 00:05:39,361
tide.

71
00:05:41,120 --> 00:05:47,820
In the 16th century B.C., Egyptian fishermen were fishing from canoes with barbed hooks

72
00:05:47,821 --> 00:05:53,800
made of brines and a variety of nets to scoop and corral fish in large numbers.

73
00:05:56,240 --> 00:06:00,200
Commercial fishing began on an international scale in 12th century Europe

74
00:06:00,201 --> 00:06:05,500
when the major maritime nations began venturing farther from their heavily fished seas.

75
00:06:07,140 --> 00:06:13,840
In 1497, Italian mariner John Cabot was contracted by England's Henry VII

76
00:06:13,841 --> 00:06:17,880
to find the passage to Asia that had eluded Christopher Columbus.

77
00:06:19,440 --> 00:06:24,360
Instead of a new trade route, Cabot found a landmass he named Newfoundland.

78
00:06:25,980 --> 00:06:30,780
Along this foreign shore, he discovered a treasure greater than gold, cod.

79
00:06:31,140 --> 00:06:36,800
And reports went back to Europe of incredible stocks of cod.

80
00:06:37,380 --> 00:06:42,160
And the phrase that was used was that you could scoop them up with a basket.

81
00:06:42,161 --> 00:06:54,300
And when Europeans heard about this, it created a kind of a rush, like a gold rush, a cod rush, to North America.

82
00:06:55,400 --> 00:06:58,100
Cod was one of the most valuable fish in the world.

83
00:06:59,240 --> 00:07:01,620
Its flavorful white flesh was highly prized.

84
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:09,780
Salting and drying the flesh could preserve it for months, yielding a food that was 80% concentrated protein.

85
00:07:09,781 --> 00:07:13,421
A good fishery was like a good oil field.

86
00:07:13,422 --> 00:07:18,660
Tremendous profits were being made from North American cod.

87
00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:32,900
So it was ideal for explorers, for provisions for armies, and in fact it became viewed by the British and by other countries as a strategically important material,

88
00:07:33,215 --> 00:07:34,620
material of war.

89
00:07:34,621 --> 00:07:41,760
Soon the French, British, Spanish and Portuguese were building long-range fishing fleets

90
00:07:41,761 --> 00:07:46,240
To begin an all-out commercial assault on the North Atlantic Cod

91
00:07:46,241 --> 00:07:50,600
The cod ranged from the waters of Newfoundland's Graham Banks

92
00:07:50,601 --> 00:07:53,960
All the way to what is now Cape Cod, Massachusetts

93
00:07:53,961 --> 00:07:58,480
The salt codfish that soon was being caught in great quantity

94
00:07:58,481 --> 00:08:00,540
And shipped back to Europe

95
00:08:00,541 --> 00:08:04,860
was being traded with the Spanish for their gold

96
00:08:04,861 --> 00:08:10,520
because salt codfish was the only protein that you could preserve in those latitudes down there.

97
00:08:11,020 --> 00:08:15,220
The technology of fishing in open seas changed little for centuries.

98
00:08:16,300 --> 00:08:18,020
Cod was generally caught by hand lining,

99
00:08:18,620 --> 00:08:23,520
a method by which one man used a line, a weight, and a baited hook

100
00:08:23,521 --> 00:08:26,000
to catch and haul in a single fish.

101
00:08:26,001 --> 00:08:30,540
Handlining from dories dates back to the 16th century.

102
00:08:32,620 --> 00:08:38,820
A dory is a small rowboat, lowered over the side of the mothership to return only when full of fish.

103
00:08:40,300 --> 00:08:42,920
Commercial dory fishing was extremely dangerous.

104
00:08:43,780 --> 00:08:47,020
A storm would come up and the dory would get blown downwind from the schooner.

105
00:08:47,720 --> 00:08:49,340
And if it was a little foggy, it would be out of sight.

106
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:53,560
And they would be adrift 300 or 400 miles from land with essentially no food.

107
00:08:54,300 --> 00:08:55,380
And, you know, that was it.

108
00:08:55,860 --> 00:09:02,380
Often the catch would get so heavy that at a certain point the small boat would just sink.

109
00:09:02,840 --> 00:09:05,800
The mothership would try to fish these guys out,

110
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:11,540
but in the North Atlantic most of the year you can't stay alive more than a few minutes in the water.

111
00:09:12,440 --> 00:09:14,280
So it was incredibly dangerous.

112
00:09:16,220 --> 00:09:20,380
But the rich cod stocks became vital to colonial America's economy.

113
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:25,060
New Englanders made immense profits trading salt cod to the world.

114
00:09:25,380 --> 00:09:31,060
It eventually brought financial independence from the British, and therefore helped establish

115
00:09:31,061 --> 00:09:33,700
the United States of America.

116
00:09:33,701 --> 00:09:41,020
But the march of technology finally shifted the worldwide demand from salted fish to fresh

117
00:09:41,021 --> 00:09:43,520
fish.

118
00:09:43,521 --> 00:09:49,260
In the late 19th century, the birth of steam power put engines aboard ships.

119
00:09:49,261 --> 00:09:54,860
Soon steam and eventually diesel powered fishing vessels were plying the oceans and scooping

120
00:09:54,861 --> 00:10:00,360
up fish in large trawl nets, effectively speeding up the delivery of the catch to market.

121
00:10:03,120 --> 00:10:08,920
Then, in the 1920s, a New York inventor named Clarence Birdseye changed fishing forever

122
00:10:08,921 --> 00:10:12,020
when he unveiled a fast freezing technique to the world.

123
00:10:12,980 --> 00:10:18,820
The fast freezing process completely changed fishing because it meant that fish could be

124
00:10:18,821 --> 00:10:23,060
taken inland in a country like the United States with a huge interior.

125
00:10:23,061 --> 00:10:26,680
This meant a tremendous increase in the market.

126
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:33,780
By 1948, the world's commercial fish catch reached 19 million tons.

127
00:10:34,900 --> 00:10:40,940
It jumped to 60 million tons in 1960, and 100 million tons by 1989.

128
00:10:43,480 --> 00:10:49,040
Hundreds of years of relentless fishing in the North Atlantic resulted in catastrophe.

129
00:10:49,041 --> 00:10:54,720
In 1992, Canada declared a moratorium on the Graham Banks Cod fishery.

130
00:10:55,520 --> 00:11:01,000
One of the most productive fisheries for over 500 years was now depleted.

131
00:11:02,660 --> 00:11:06,480
While overfishing in most of the world's oceans is now a reality,

132
00:11:07,620 --> 00:11:11,240
a few conservation-minded scientists are fighting back

133
00:11:11,241 --> 00:11:16,760
by using behavior modification to keep juvenile fish out of the net entirely.

134
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:24,440
Paul Revere used the image of a codfish, a symbol of prosperity, when he designed currency

135
00:11:24,441 --> 00:11:29,040
plates for the Massachusetts Bay Colony in 1776.

136
00:11:29,041 --> 00:11:32,040
Commercial fishing will return.

137
00:11:32,041 --> 00:11:42,040
The Port of Gloucester, Massachusetts is the oldest fishing harbor in the United States.

138
00:11:42,041 --> 00:11:47,560
Its seafarers have fished the far-off waters of Georgia's Bank and the Grand Banks since

139
00:11:47,561 --> 00:11:51,694
the 17th century, many never returned.

140
00:11:51,695 --> 00:11:57,460
Overlooking the harbor is the Fisherman's Memorial, a

141
00:11:57,461 --> 00:12:03,220
tribute naming thousands of local fishermen who have lost their lives at sea, including

142
00:12:03,221 --> 00:12:08,185
the six men who perished in the infamous Halloween Gale in 1991.

143
00:12:08,186 --> 00:12:10,280
Their story was told in the

144
00:12:10,281 --> 00:12:14,501
book and the 2000 feature film, The Perfect Storm.

145
00:12:14,502 --> 00:12:17,540
Linda Greenlaw was on the outer edge

146
00:12:17,541 --> 00:12:22,460
of the perfect storm, skippering the Hannah Bowden, the Andrea Gale's sister ship.

147
00:12:22,780 --> 00:12:25,768
We had 70 knots of wind for a couple of days. It was bad.

148
00:12:25,769 --> 00:12:27,760
It was not life-threatening. But I was

149
00:12:27,761 --> 00:12:28,461
very frightened.

150
00:12:28,462 --> 00:12:31,520
Listening to the boats west of me, these are guys who've been fishing all their

151
00:12:31,521 --> 00:12:38,220
lives. The Andrea Gale eventually succumbed to the 100-foot seas and 90-knot winds.

152
00:12:39,580 --> 00:12:43,860
Commercial fishing is always on the top of the list of the most dangerous professions.

153
00:12:43,861 --> 00:12:46,200
and a lot of that, of course, is due to bad weather.

154
00:12:48,820 --> 00:12:52,640
Commercial fishing is one of the toughest professions on the face of the planet.

155
00:12:53,320 --> 00:12:57,140
These are individuals who go hundreds of miles away.

156
00:12:57,400 --> 00:12:59,320
They work around the clock.

157
00:12:59,480 --> 00:13:05,640
They operate under changing conditions, often under horrendous conditions.

158
00:13:06,540 --> 00:13:11,320
And they really are, I guess, the last of the true hunter-gatherers.

159
00:13:11,321 --> 00:13:17,080
Far from shore, smaller boats are more susceptible to nature's fury.

160
00:13:19,040 --> 00:13:24,080
In fishing ports up and down the east coast, many of these small boats are called draggers.

161
00:13:25,740 --> 00:13:30,660
Draggers are bottom trawlers, vessels that tow a large net along the ocean bottom,

162
00:13:31,680 --> 00:13:36,400
targeting ground fish, such as haddock, cod, whiting, and flounder.

163
00:13:36,401 --> 00:13:42,940
Today's bottom trawler pulls the funnel-shaped net at a three-knot average speed.

164
00:13:44,200 --> 00:13:46,900
Guiding the net on either end are otter doors.

165
00:13:47,660 --> 00:13:50,260
These heavy metal devices force the net to the bottom

166
00:13:50,261 --> 00:13:54,640
and provide hydrodynamic spread to keep the net's mouth open.

167
00:13:56,160 --> 00:13:59,780
Floats positioned along the headline keep the net open vertically,

168
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:05,540
while a weighted ground rope and rubber rollers keep the net in contact with the ocean bottom.

169
00:14:06,400 --> 00:14:13,400
As fish are overtaken, they're guided into the collection area, known as the cod end.

170
00:14:13,401 --> 00:14:20,400
While popular, bottom trawlers are outnumbered by vessels called purse saeners.

171
00:14:20,401 --> 00:14:27,400
Saeners target high and mid-water schooling fish, such as tuna, salmon and mackerel.

172
00:14:27,401 --> 00:14:33,400
In past centuries, saen nets were deployed from beaches.

173
00:14:33,401 --> 00:14:37,400
Fishermen rode the long nets in a semicircle around a school of fish,

174
00:14:37,401 --> 00:14:40,400
while horses pulled to catch to shore.

175
00:14:43,400 --> 00:14:47,400
Today's open ocean purse seins can reach a length of one mile.

176
00:14:48,400 --> 00:14:51,400
The net is motored around a large school of fish.

177
00:14:54,400 --> 00:14:56,400
When the circle is complete,

178
00:14:56,401 --> 00:15:00,400
the bottom purse line is pulled in like the strings of a woman's purse,

179
00:15:00,401 --> 00:15:02,400
trapping the fish.

180
00:15:03,400 --> 00:15:10,400
The net is tightened and the fish are removed and brought aboard for cold storage.

181
00:15:15,400 --> 00:15:22,400
Purse seining and bottom dragging are highly effective, but at the same time pose environmental threats.

182
00:15:22,401 --> 00:15:25,400
The primary threat is known as bycatch.

183
00:15:25,401 --> 00:15:30,400
If you put a net into the water you're going to catch a number of things that you are not targeting

184
00:15:30,401 --> 00:15:36,400
targeting and then those are thrown back into the water at the end of the

185
00:15:36,401 --> 00:15:43,400
toe. Globally we waste some 30% of everything that's caught that is not targeted.

186
00:15:46,400 --> 00:15:52,400
In eastern Pacific waters by the 1960s, vast numbers of bycatch dolphins were

187
00:15:52,401 --> 00:15:57,400
dying in purse sains meant for tuna. Because dolphins often school above the

188
00:15:57,401 --> 00:16:03,400
fish. Fishermen targeted the mammals to catch tuna.

189
00:16:03,401 --> 00:16:08,760
Public outcry in the United States eventually pressured Congress into passing the 1990 Dolphin

190
00:16:08,761 --> 00:16:09,461
Safe Act.

191
00:16:09,462 --> 00:16:15,280
Today the label indicates that government observers certify the tuna has been caught

192
00:16:15,281 --> 00:16:17,149
without harming dolphins.

193
00:16:17,150 --> 00:16:21,860
This is achieved by physically lowering the float rope to allow

194
00:16:21,861 --> 00:16:25,900
the mammals to escape the net.

195
00:16:25,901 --> 00:16:31,000
While the Dolphin Safe Program has been very successful, limiting other types of bycatch

196
00:16:31,001 --> 00:16:34,040
is often more difficult.

197
00:16:34,041 --> 00:16:40,800
The National Marine Fisheries Service monitors commercial catch quotas and bycatch limits.

198
00:16:40,801 --> 00:16:43,980
But scientists and fishermen often don't see eye to eye.

199
00:16:43,981 --> 00:16:47,360
This impacts their way they make a living, their profitability.

200
00:16:47,361 --> 00:16:51,340
And there was a lot of confrontation, I think, for a long time between us and them.

201
00:16:51,540 --> 00:16:52,560
It was us versus them.

202
00:16:53,160 --> 00:16:55,820
And they're on the water every day, and they see things.

203
00:16:56,040 --> 00:16:59,760
And so we started what's called cooperative research, working with the industry,

204
00:16:59,960 --> 00:17:02,120
and it's become a more open process.

205
00:17:03,220 --> 00:17:06,780
Cooperative research pairs actual commercial fishermen with scientists

206
00:17:06,781 --> 00:17:10,480
to develop new conservation-minded fishing techniques.

207
00:17:10,481 --> 00:17:18,200
Most recently, this partnership has resolved a highly publicized emotional bycatch issue,

208
00:17:18,880 --> 00:17:20,720
the fate of the endangered sea turtle.

209
00:17:21,820 --> 00:17:25,220
For many years, shrimp trawlers were responsible for the catch,

210
00:17:25,720 --> 00:17:28,620
and often the subsequent deaths of various sea turtle species.

211
00:17:30,500 --> 00:17:34,020
The National Marine Fisheries Service responded with a device

212
00:17:34,021 --> 00:17:36,780
that has now required gear on shrimp trawl nets.

213
00:17:38,180 --> 00:17:40,420
It's called a turtle excluder device.

214
00:17:40,480 --> 00:17:43,061
Basically it's very simple. It's a grid.

215
00:17:43,062 --> 00:17:46,480
It's usually made out of aluminum or stainless steel.

216
00:17:46,481 --> 00:17:48,938
It goes into the nets. You put a hole below it.

217
00:17:48,939 --> 00:17:54,480
And so what it does is the turtle hits that grid and just shoots out of the net, either top or the bottom.

218
00:17:54,481 --> 00:17:57,480
But the grids are big enough so the fish go through.

219
00:18:00,480 --> 00:18:09,480
The Marine Conservation Program at Massachusetts Manamit Center is dedicated to reducing bycatch in the problematic New England fisheries.

220
00:18:09,481 --> 00:18:16,860
So what we have been trying to do over the years is to develop more targeted, more selective

221
00:18:16,861 --> 00:18:21,580
fishing practices that allow us to catch only the things that we want to catch, that there's

222
00:18:21,581 --> 00:18:25,800
a market for and release everything else underwater unharmed.

223
00:18:25,801 --> 00:18:31,140
The New England haddock fishery has long been plagued by the unwanted bycatch of the severely

224
00:18:31,141 --> 00:18:33,660
depleted Atlantic cod.

225
00:18:33,661 --> 00:18:38,580
The two fish frequently swim in close contact.

226
00:18:38,581 --> 00:18:43,980
In fixed underwater video cameras, Manamit scientists studied the behavior of cod and

227
00:18:43,981 --> 00:18:46,720
haddock as they entered the net.

228
00:18:46,721 --> 00:18:50,540
They found that haddock usually react by swimming higher in the water.

229
00:18:50,541 --> 00:18:54,640
So if they enter the net at a higher level, then we're able to put a separator panel in

230
00:18:54,641 --> 00:18:59,100
the middle of the net, which will direct all of the haddock into the cod end where we retain

231
00:18:59,101 --> 00:19:02,600
the fish, and all the cod will go out underneath.

232
00:19:02,601 --> 00:19:08,560
A more far-reaching problem is the by-catch of all species of juvenile fish.

233
00:19:08,561 --> 00:19:15,560
forbid catching small young fish because of the potential damage to the stocks population.

234
00:19:17,560 --> 00:19:22,220
A larger net mesh size has helped curb some bycatch by allowing juveniles to escape the

235
00:19:22,221 --> 00:19:28,220
net. But scientists are now trying to improve results with behavior modification.

236
00:19:28,221 --> 00:19:34,220
We know that most of the reactions of the fish are mediated through the visual system.

237
00:19:34,221 --> 00:19:37,680
so the fish see what's around them, they try to avoid it.

238
00:19:38,740 --> 00:19:42,780
We inserted into the net something that we think would look like

239
00:19:42,781 --> 00:19:45,520
the large looming mouth of an approaching predator,

240
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,900
so we simply put a black tarp inside the net.

241
00:19:49,720 --> 00:19:54,160
It causes all of the fish that encounter it to try and escape through the meshes.

242
00:19:54,940 --> 00:19:59,160
The small ones are capable of doing it because they're small enough to get out of the meshes.

243
00:19:59,161 --> 00:20:07,000
While still in its infancy, fish behavior modification is offering promising solutions to the bycatch problem.

244
00:20:08,460 --> 00:20:11,940
This fish psychotherapy may someday go high tech.

245
00:20:12,580 --> 00:20:20,660
This may involve things like using laser lights to direct fish to a much smaller cod end

246
00:20:20,661 --> 00:20:22,600
so that we're not towing a whole net.

247
00:20:22,601 --> 00:20:29,460
or we might be able to use holograms of some description to get fish to react to those.

248
00:20:29,800 --> 00:20:33,400
There are lots of things that we haven't even dreamt of yet.

249
00:20:34,080 --> 00:20:38,120
While scientists are learning how to better preserve wild fish species,

250
00:20:39,020 --> 00:20:44,700
others are domesticating them in an effort to build the high-tech fish farm of the future.

251
00:20:44,701 --> 00:20:55,000
Since the year 1716, more than 5,000 fishermen from the port of Gloucester, Massachusetts, have died at sea.

252
00:20:55,960 --> 00:20:58,600
Commercial fishing will return on Modern Marvels.

253
00:21:02,380 --> 00:21:10,740
For thousands of years, commercial fishing followed a hunter-gatherer tradition, pursuing and harvesting fish in the wild.

254
00:21:11,600 --> 00:21:18,680
But an explosion in world population and the reality of overfishing have promoted a newer vision of the commercial fisherman.

255
00:21:19,620 --> 00:21:22,640
This fisherman doesn't catch fish. He farms it.

256
00:21:24,260 --> 00:21:28,580
In 1970, U.S. commercial aquaculture was in its infancy,

257
00:21:29,140 --> 00:21:35,180
with most farms raising trout, catfish, or carp in inland ponds or holding tanks.

258
00:21:35,940 --> 00:21:39,880
Worldwide aquaculture contributed just over 3% of all fish production.

259
00:21:39,881 --> 00:21:44,880
By the year 2003, it was contributing more than 30%.

260
00:21:44,881 --> 00:21:50,880
In the 21st century, the salmon is the king of the aquatic barnyard.

261
00:21:50,881 --> 00:21:54,880
The near-shore farming of salmon is a booming business,

262
00:21:54,881 --> 00:21:59,880
with vast underwater cage complexes located in calm water bays and estuaries

263
00:21:59,881 --> 00:22:04,880
in places like Norway, Chile and the coast of Maine.

264
00:22:04,881 --> 00:22:08,880
But large-scale near-shore farming isn't the perfect solution.

265
00:22:08,881 --> 00:22:12,121
solution. The environment could be the casualty.

266
00:22:12,122 --> 00:22:15,160
Salmon raised in pens are raised in very high

267
00:22:15,161 --> 00:22:15,861
densities.

268
00:22:15,862 --> 00:22:18,380
And just like with human beings, when you have a lot of people together, a

269
00:22:18,381 --> 00:22:20,900
lot of fish together, disease tends to run rampant.

270
00:22:20,901 --> 00:22:22,580
And then these diseases get out to

271
00:22:22,581 --> 00:22:28,059
the wild populations and can cause them significant physiological stress.

272
00:22:28,060 --> 00:22:29,560
A more typical issue

273
00:22:29,561 --> 00:22:32,440
is dealing with huge amounts of fish waste.

274
00:22:32,441 --> 00:22:35,320
Salmon aquaculturists need places that have

275
00:22:35,321 --> 00:22:38,400
clean water with very little pollution.

276
00:22:38,401 --> 00:22:44,360
The irony of needing these clean water places for salmon is that salmon then in turn end

277
00:22:44,361 --> 00:22:50,620
up polluting the very water that was chosen for its pristine nature.

278
00:22:50,621 --> 00:22:54,160
The impacts that were noticed were primarily impacts on the seafloor.

279
00:22:54,161 --> 00:23:01,940
So fish feces, uneaten food, settling on the seafloor, changing what was a natural environment

280
00:23:01,941 --> 00:23:04,700
into an environment that was degraded.

281
00:23:05,320 --> 00:23:11,320
Salmon farmers are sensitive to these problems, and attempts have been made to reduce environmental

282
00:23:11,321 --> 00:23:12,021
impacts.

283
00:23:12,022 --> 00:23:18,320
But with 28% of the world's wild fish stocks either overfished or nearing extinction,

284
00:23:18,321 --> 00:23:24,320
aquaculture must overcome its drawbacks to help feed an expanding world.

285
00:23:24,321 --> 00:23:31,320
The National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration, or NOAA, has vowed to increase the annual

286
00:23:31,321 --> 00:23:32,853
annual value of U.S.

287
00:23:32,854 --> 00:23:37,760
fish farming from $1 billion to $5 billion in the next 25 years.

288
00:23:37,761 --> 00:23:39,567
We have to look at the combination.

289
00:23:39,568 --> 00:23:42,200
How does the wild and the agriculture fit together?

290
00:23:42,201 --> 00:23:46,300
So there's no doubt that agriculture has a place in the future. Is it controversial?

291
00:23:46,301 --> 00:23:51,460
You bet. Right now it is extremely controversial.

292
00:23:51,461 --> 00:23:57,515
One group of scientists is going where no fish farmer has dared to cultivate.

293
00:23:57,516 --> 00:23:58,380
Researchers

294
00:23:58,381 --> 00:24:03,420
with the University of New Hampshire's Open Ocean Aquaculture program hope to neutralize

295
00:24:03,421 --> 00:24:08,340
some of the environmental impacts by moving the farm miles out to sea.

296
00:24:08,341 --> 00:24:11,700
I think the offshore environment is actually a better environment for a lot of the species

297
00:24:11,701 --> 00:24:13,800
that we're thinking about culturing.

298
00:24:13,801 --> 00:24:16,600
You've got a lot more stable conditions in terms

299
00:24:16,601 --> 00:24:21,380
of the temperature and salinity, you've got a lot of water movement so that there's always

300
00:24:21,381 --> 00:24:23,544
clean water and well oxygenated water.

301
00:24:23,545 --> 00:24:26,220
And on the environmental side of things you get

302
00:24:26,221 --> 00:24:33,160
a lot more dispersion of any kind of waste, both fish waste and any uneaten food.

303
00:24:33,161 --> 00:24:38,040
After years of experiments with flounder and haddock in smaller cages, the team has built

304
00:24:38,041 --> 00:24:44,280
the first commercial scale farm, containing over 35,000 of the famous Atlantic cod.

305
00:24:44,281 --> 00:24:50,120
But the scientific challenges of building an open ocean cage that could withstand the

306
00:24:50,121 --> 00:24:52,720
biggest New England storms were daunting.

307
00:24:52,721 --> 00:24:58,760
When it came to mooring systems, we took an approach of developing modeling tools

308
00:24:58,761 --> 00:25:03,860
so that we could understand how you would attach these things to the seafloor,

309
00:25:03,960 --> 00:25:07,440
what kind of anchors, what kind of ropes, where the stresses would be.

310
00:25:08,680 --> 00:25:15,240
Three 3,000 cubic meter cages were assembled by divers and sunk to a depth of 100 feet.

311
00:25:16,580 --> 00:25:21,540
Nine one-ton anchors and heavy-duty chain hold the deep-sea farm in place.

312
00:25:22,720 --> 00:25:30,080
Once the farm was up and running, thousands of cod fingerlings began their two-year lease in the blue water cages.

313
00:25:32,080 --> 00:25:39,940
But raising the brood of wild cod in captivity was uncharted territory, so video cameras were installed to monitor the nursery.

314
00:25:41,300 --> 00:25:46,800
The deep-sea cameras are controlled remotely to observe the societal behaviors of the captive cod.

315
00:25:48,060 --> 00:25:52,660
The researchers implanted small sonar transmitters inside a number of fish.

316
00:25:52,720 --> 00:25:56,920
And then we have hydrophones or listening devices inside the cage,

317
00:25:57,060 --> 00:26:00,240
and we can then track these fish 24 hours a day,

318
00:26:00,360 --> 00:26:03,560
track their motion throughout the cage, not only where they go,

319
00:26:03,800 --> 00:26:08,500
but how fast they're swimming and understand what their behavior is

320
00:26:08,501 --> 00:26:11,400
and what their swimming speed is relative to feeding,

321
00:26:11,680 --> 00:26:16,320
relative to day-night differences, relative to interactions with other fish.

322
00:26:18,240 --> 00:26:21,100
Because the farm is over 10 miles from shore,

323
00:26:21,920 --> 00:26:25,140
Feeding the cod requires the latest in high-tech room service.

324
00:26:27,240 --> 00:26:30,420
A lone automated feeder buoy sustains the colony.

325
00:26:32,840 --> 00:26:35,660
By periodically stocking the buoy with fish pellets,

326
00:26:36,780 --> 00:26:40,480
the researchers are able to pump measured quantities of feed into the cages.

327
00:26:43,480 --> 00:26:47,360
Inside the buoy is the remote-controlled brain that rings the dinner bell.

328
00:26:47,361 --> 00:26:54,360
So we've got to have all the control mechanisms, all the solenoid valves and so on that open

329
00:26:54,620 --> 00:26:59,720
and close all these valves, turn the pumps on, so it's really a giant computer that's

330
00:26:59,721 --> 00:27:02,445
out there operating all these mechanical systems.

331
00:27:02,446 --> 00:27:04,780
And what's nice about it is we can do that

332
00:27:04,781 --> 00:27:07,640
from the computer here at the university.

333
00:27:07,641 --> 00:27:13,835
The Open Ocean Aquaculture Project represents a quantum leap in fish farming.

334
00:27:13,836 --> 00:27:14,640
But it too

335
00:27:14,641 --> 00:27:18,740
potential environmental impacts.

336
00:27:18,741 --> 00:27:22,280
One of the greatest challenges is oxygen depletion.

337
00:27:22,281 --> 00:27:25,960
Farm-raised fish excrete ammonia, which depletes oxygen.

338
00:27:25,961 --> 00:27:28,840
Well right now we can't measure it beyond the rim of the cage.

339
00:27:28,841 --> 00:27:31,360
We haven't seen any oxygen depletion.

340
00:27:31,361 --> 00:27:34,320
But then again, we have 35,000 fish out there.

341
00:27:34,321 --> 00:27:38,120
The farm of the future will have a half a million fish.

342
00:27:38,121 --> 00:27:41,760
Understanding what happens on a small scale and then building it out is going to help

343
00:27:41,761 --> 00:27:46,040
us predict what happens in a real commercial operation.

344
00:27:46,041 --> 00:27:51,340
But even as fish farms get bigger, the world will continue to depend on wild caught fish

345
00:27:51,341 --> 00:27:53,435
to feed its growing masses.

346
00:27:53,436 --> 00:27:58,480
And in one remote fishery, there is a factory at sea that without

347
00:27:58,481 --> 00:28:05,160
restraint has the capability to strip the oceans of their fish.

348
00:28:05,161 --> 00:28:11,360
Growing seaweed in a fish farm can improve water quality by reducing nitrogen and phosphorus,

349
00:28:11,361 --> 00:28:13,840
both found in fish waste.

350
00:28:13,841 --> 00:28:22,280
Commercial fishing will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

351
00:28:22,281 --> 00:28:29,040
Hundreds of miles from shore, in frigid Alaskan seas that can produce wave heights of 50 feet,

352
00:28:29,041 --> 00:28:34,880
the modern factory trawler gives chase to a school of pollen.

353
00:28:34,881 --> 00:28:40,180
Some of these massive catcher processors have the ability to catch, process and freeze more

354
00:28:40,181 --> 00:28:42,780
than 500 tons of fish in a single day.

355
00:28:42,781 --> 00:28:49,300
It's one of the reasons that a vessel like this is so large, is that we take our production

356
00:28:49,301 --> 00:28:54,460
facility to the fish, rather than bringing the fish to the production facility.

357
00:28:54,461 --> 00:28:59,740
While salmon was and is still a lucrative Alaskan fish, for decades few considered the

358
00:28:59,741 --> 00:29:05,800
boundless offshore populations of walleye pollock, Pacific cod and whiting in the Gulf

359
00:29:05,801 --> 00:29:07,300
and Bering Sea.

360
00:29:07,301 --> 00:29:15,600
In the 1950s, factory trawler fleets from the Soviet Union, Japan, and other countries began fishing these remote waters.

361
00:29:17,600 --> 00:29:24,740
As time went on, and other people here in the Northwest understood that those fisheries were worth a lot of money,

362
00:29:25,480 --> 00:29:29,820
and that we could bring a lot more of those dollars home to the beach, home to America,

363
00:29:29,960 --> 00:29:32,860
if we extended our jurisdiction out to the 200 mile.

364
00:29:33,320 --> 00:29:37,020
It wasn't just a fisheries decision, but it was a good decision for fishermen.

365
00:29:37,300 --> 00:29:43,780
Until 1976, most nations observed a 12-mile territorial boundary at sea.

366
00:29:44,660 --> 00:29:46,980
Then Congress passed the Magnuson Act,

367
00:29:47,540 --> 00:29:51,400
and the U.S. established a 200-mile exclusive economic zone.

368
00:29:52,520 --> 00:29:53,860
So did many other nations.

369
00:29:54,780 --> 00:29:57,380
The U.S. proceeded to evict the foreign fleets.

370
00:29:58,600 --> 00:30:03,020
At first, American fishermen worked in joint ventures with foreign factory vessels.

371
00:30:03,021 --> 00:30:08,840
until the early 1980s, when the U.S. launched its own factory trawler fleet.

372
00:30:09,740 --> 00:30:12,360
When those vessels began coming around,

373
00:30:13,200 --> 00:30:16,660
we began essentially taking over the entire fishery

374
00:30:16,661 --> 00:30:19,920
and moving the foreign fleets off the 200-mile zone

375
00:30:19,921 --> 00:30:24,540
and putting up a whole new kind of fishing in Alaska that we hadn't seen there before.

376
00:30:26,720 --> 00:30:28,880
Based in Seattle's Puget Sound,

377
00:30:28,881 --> 00:30:33,880
Today's fleet of catcher processors travel to Alaskan waters for two fishing seasons,

378
00:30:33,881 --> 00:30:37,880
in the winter and late summer.

379
00:30:37,881 --> 00:30:44,880
While there are only 15 active vessels in the entire fleet, their catch capacity is unrivaled.

380
00:30:44,881 --> 00:30:52,880
In 2002, the factory trawlers and smaller catcher vessels landed more than 1.5 million tons of pollock,

381
00:30:52,881 --> 00:30:57,880
some 40% of the entire commercial fish catch in the United States.

382
00:30:57,881 --> 00:31:03,100
At the time that these big vessels were being developed,

383
00:31:03,380 --> 00:31:07,880
there were great technological advances going on in hydraulics,

384
00:31:07,940 --> 00:31:11,680
high-pressure hydraulics, gear, big troll winches,

385
00:31:12,300 --> 00:31:13,620
and certainly electronics.

386
00:31:14,100 --> 00:31:16,540
Also in the material that the nets were made out of,

387
00:31:16,620 --> 00:31:20,700
spectrofiber nets, very similar to Kevlar

388
00:31:20,701 --> 00:31:23,420
and things that you would make bulletproof vests out of.

389
00:31:24,360 --> 00:31:27,080
With a price tag approaching $100,000,

390
00:31:27,940 --> 00:31:33,340
Some of these mid-water monstrosities have a mouth nearly as wide as a football field is long.

391
00:31:35,140 --> 00:31:39,420
The Alaska Fisheries Science Center makes and repairs a variety of trawl nets.

392
00:31:40,380 --> 00:31:43,880
This is a four-seam bottom trawl using the ground fish fisheries in Alaska.

393
00:31:44,660 --> 00:31:47,960
This net is towed by a boat of about 700 horsepower.

394
00:31:48,460 --> 00:31:50,140
Typical factory trawler in Alaska.

395
00:31:50,640 --> 00:31:54,100
It's closer to 2,500 to 3,500 horsepower.

396
00:31:54,101 --> 00:31:59,800
This is a lot smaller than you'll see on those boats, but it's of the same shape, just a smaller version of it.

397
00:32:00,440 --> 00:32:05,080
Protecting many trawl nets from damage and maximizing their spread radius underwater

398
00:32:05,081 --> 00:32:11,520
is the duty of wireless acoustic sensors mounted on or near the otter doors that pull the net.

399
00:32:12,540 --> 00:32:16,960
What these sensors do is send back information about the geometry of the trawl.

400
00:32:17,380 --> 00:32:18,620
These are door spread sensors.

401
00:32:18,621 --> 00:32:22,200
One of them is placed on either door, and you can get the distance between it,

402
00:32:22,280 --> 00:32:24,560
and you can see that your trawl is flying stable.

403
00:32:24,800 --> 00:32:28,060
And this increases the efficiency of the fishing operation drastically.

404
00:32:30,900 --> 00:32:34,940
This pollock fishery, fortunately, is the cleanest fishery in the world.

405
00:32:35,260 --> 00:32:38,440
And we catch almost 100% pollock.

406
00:32:38,600 --> 00:32:41,920
So it's very important for us, too, to keep it clean.

407
00:32:43,560 --> 00:32:47,060
Fishermen often refer to the cod end of the net as the money bag.

408
00:32:48,620 --> 00:32:54,600
Knowing when the giant bag is full under water is critical to avoid smashing the fish with an oversized load.

409
00:32:56,340 --> 00:32:58,500
These are catch load sensors right here.

410
00:32:59,060 --> 00:33:03,160
And the way that these work are as the bag fills up, naturally it gets wider this way.

411
00:33:03,560 --> 00:33:05,540
These lines start taking some strain.

412
00:33:05,940 --> 00:33:08,020
And you'll see this, oftentimes it's called a sausage.

413
00:33:08,560 --> 00:33:12,740
As that happens and these open up sideways, it pulls and it trips this

414
00:33:12,741 --> 00:33:18,020
and sends an acoustic message back to the skipper and says that at this point there's fish up to this point.

415
00:33:18,021 --> 00:33:18,721
in the bag.

416
00:33:18,722 --> 00:33:23,100
And when this happens, the skipper knows that I have a 100-ton bag, I've got

417
00:33:23,101 --> 00:33:26,660
100 tons of fish, and it's time to haul back before I cause damage to the net.

418
00:33:27,820 --> 00:33:33,620
Because the processing factory below deck runs 24 hours a day, fish must be caught at

419
00:33:33,621 --> 00:33:37,000
a rate that matches the factory's ability to process and freeze it.

420
00:33:38,620 --> 00:33:44,200
Automation for us is of the essence. It's extremely important because we don't have

421
00:33:44,201 --> 00:33:46,380
a lot of room on board for people.

422
00:33:46,960 --> 00:33:48,640
We're limited by the amount of people

423
00:33:48,641 --> 00:33:50,260
we can put on a vessel this size.

424
00:33:50,400 --> 00:33:52,780
So anything we can automate, we do.

425
00:33:53,900 --> 00:33:56,180
The vessels that we operate run somewhere between

426
00:33:56,181 --> 00:33:58,420
15 and 20 tons of fish an hour

427
00:33:58,421 --> 00:34:01,500
and on a pace that produces roughly

428
00:34:01,501 --> 00:34:03,440
100 tons of finished goods a day.

429
00:34:05,120 --> 00:34:07,260
After sizing and weighing the catch,

430
00:34:07,261 --> 00:34:09,480
the removal of skin and bones begins.

431
00:34:10,140 --> 00:34:12,420
We can process 120 fish a minute

432
00:34:12,421 --> 00:34:15,680
through a fillet machine that will produce a boneless, skinless fillet.

433
00:34:16,500 --> 00:34:20,120
And that's done without any interaction with the fish

434
00:34:20,121 --> 00:34:22,200
other than the driver placing it into the machine.

435
00:34:23,040 --> 00:34:26,540
So sizing of the fish and then a proper adjustment of the fillet machine

436
00:34:26,541 --> 00:34:28,040
is a critical thing for us.

437
00:34:28,780 --> 00:34:33,380
The human eye checks the machine's work as the fillets cross the candling table.

438
00:34:34,240 --> 00:34:37,080
Here, workers check for any skin or bones that remain.

439
00:34:38,240 --> 00:34:42,160
From this point, the fish will become one of two products for the end consumer.

440
00:34:42,420 --> 00:34:48,820
The majority are pressed into formed fish fillets that will end up as fast food sandwiches

441
00:34:48,821 --> 00:34:51,280
or fish sticks.

442
00:34:51,281 --> 00:34:53,280
The other product is called Surimi.

443
00:34:53,281 --> 00:35:00,540
Surimi was originally created as a form of storage for fish without refrigeration.

444
00:35:00,541 --> 00:35:06,320
The process of creating Surimi is a process that removes the soluble protein and oils

445
00:35:06,321 --> 00:35:09,000
from the fish protein.

446
00:35:09,001 --> 00:35:13,840
This fish paste is later used to make imitation crab meat and many other manufactured foods

447
00:35:13,841 --> 00:35:19,000
in the Japanese market.

448
00:35:19,001 --> 00:35:23,920
When onboard processing is complete, the fish products are quickly frozen and then delivered

449
00:35:23,921 --> 00:35:26,660
to the ship's massive cold storage hold.

450
00:35:26,661 --> 00:35:33,620
When the hold is full, the fishing ends and the vessel heads to port for unloading.

451
00:35:33,621 --> 00:35:39,420
Throughout the 1990s, a combined yearly harvest quota for Pollock turned the Bering Sea into

452
00:35:39,421 --> 00:35:42,580
a high stakes commercial fishing tournament.

453
00:35:42,581 --> 00:35:47,820
The fishery was open for everybody to fish as fast and as hard as you could until the

454
00:35:47,821 --> 00:35:50,180
quota was caught.

455
00:35:50,181 --> 00:35:55,220
There was a real pressure not only from fishermen but from the environmental community to end

456
00:35:55,221 --> 00:35:58,280
what they call the race for fish.

457
00:35:58,281 --> 00:36:05,280
But by the end of the 20th century, the Bering Sea Pollock fishery underwent a drastic change.

458
00:36:05,281 --> 00:36:12,280
Today the competitors are now teammates, as a cooperative is now in place to eliminate overfishing.

459
00:36:12,281 --> 00:36:17,280
But when more than a million tons of pollock are being harvested in a single year,

460
00:36:17,281 --> 00:36:25,280
it's up to a new generation of fishery scientists to make sure this fish will never share the fate of the Atlantic cod.

461
00:36:28,280 --> 00:36:39,880
In 1999, scientists tracked a school of pollock that covered more than 30,000 square nautical miles and contained more than 11 billion fish.

462
00:36:40,920 --> 00:36:43,820
Commercial fishing will return on Modern Marvels.

463
00:36:43,821 --> 00:36:50,020
At Seaview Lobster Company in Kittery, Maine,

464
00:36:50,320 --> 00:36:53,480
a harvest of prehistoric-looking bugs

465
00:36:53,481 --> 00:36:55,680
is sorted, weighed, and prepared

466
00:36:55,681 --> 00:36:59,240
for overnight shipment to points around the world.

467
00:37:00,840 --> 00:37:04,220
Commercial lobstering is a $300 million a year business,

468
00:37:04,720 --> 00:37:06,900
one of America's highest-value fisheries.

469
00:37:07,540 --> 00:37:10,580
But the lust for lobster has a very short history.

470
00:37:10,581 --> 00:37:14,240
Well, lobsters were always regarded as a trash species.

471
00:37:14,680 --> 00:37:17,000
Years ago, they used to wash up in windrows on the beach,

472
00:37:17,080 --> 00:37:20,560
and people would harvest them, carry them home, and spread them on the fields as fertilizer.

473
00:37:20,900 --> 00:37:22,760
They were always fed to the slaves and the servants,

474
00:37:23,000 --> 00:37:25,500
and they were considered a very low-level dietary item.

475
00:37:25,820 --> 00:37:28,720
As time progressed, people seemed to develop a taste for them.

476
00:37:28,780 --> 00:37:29,840
They became more and more popular.

477
00:37:30,840 --> 00:37:35,980
By the late 1800s, their value skyrocketed, and the great lobster hunt began.

478
00:37:36,680 --> 00:37:39,720
And then as the fishing picked up, the numbers declined.

479
00:37:39,721 --> 00:37:45,480
fairly rapidly, but the unusual thing, the surprising thing is, despite all the fishing

480
00:37:45,481 --> 00:37:50,800
pressure, the lobster populations have not declined much in the last 50 years, I'd say.

481
00:37:53,220 --> 00:37:58,940
At the University of New Hampshire, zoologists are trying to determine how lobsters are resisting

482
00:37:58,941 --> 00:38:02,760
fishing pressure in an attempt to better forecast their future.

483
00:38:02,761 --> 00:38:09,020
One part of the research uses video cameras placed inside the traps.

484
00:38:10,980 --> 00:38:13,840
The static lobster trap has changed very little.

485
00:38:14,560 --> 00:38:17,300
It still depends on the lobsters to capture themselves.

486
00:38:17,800 --> 00:38:23,580
We found out that although many lobsters approach traps, many lobsters go into traps,

487
00:38:24,180 --> 00:38:27,060
we actually end up catching very few of those lobsters.

488
00:38:27,280 --> 00:38:32,180
The time-lapse cameras revealed that the lobster fishery thrives on inefficiency.

489
00:38:32,760 --> 00:38:37,760
The most striking thing is that lobsters are climbing all over these traps.

490
00:38:37,761 --> 00:38:38,760
It's like a little anthill.

491
00:38:38,761 --> 00:38:43,760
Once a lobster gets into the trap, it prevents others from entering while it's feeding.

492
00:38:43,761 --> 00:38:45,760
They can escape fairly readily.

493
00:38:45,761 --> 00:38:46,760
Ninety percent of them get out.

494
00:38:46,761 --> 00:38:51,760
We call it the restaurant theory that they're just stopping by, they're having something to eat.

495
00:38:51,761 --> 00:38:54,760
They occasionally get caught and pulled up to the surface.

496
00:38:54,761 --> 00:38:56,760
But many, many of them get away.

497
00:38:56,761 --> 00:39:00,760
This has implications of why the fishery is so robust.

498
00:39:00,761 --> 00:39:05,360
We're not removing as many lobsters as we might be able to remove if we had very efficient traps.

499
00:39:06,500 --> 00:39:12,320
By mounting sonar transmitters on lobsters, the scientists are able to remotely monitor movement

500
00:39:12,321 --> 00:39:15,760
and gain valuable information on behavior dynamics.

501
00:39:18,600 --> 00:39:23,500
In the inefficient New England lobster fishery, catch levels continue to be high.

502
00:39:23,501 --> 00:39:31,600
But inefficiency plays no part in the $2 billion a year Alaska Pollock fishery.

503
00:39:31,601 --> 00:39:36,300
Here absolute efficiency is the key to conservation.

504
00:39:36,301 --> 00:39:43,300
The National Marine Fisheries Service has revolutionized the science of population dynamics

505
00:39:43,301 --> 00:39:46,600
and forged a rare partnership with the commercial fishing fleet.

506
00:39:46,601 --> 00:39:53,380
We conduct annual stock assessments where we estimate the productivity of the resource

507
00:39:53,381 --> 00:40:01,140
and from that make recommendations to the North Pacific Council on harvest levels for the coming year.

508
00:40:02,020 --> 00:40:07,420
Scientists estimated the pollock population at 12.3 million tons in 2004.

509
00:40:08,720 --> 00:40:15,540
Based on legal catch limits, fishermen harvested 1.64 million tons, or about 13% of the total population.

510
00:40:16,540 --> 00:40:19,520
Counting fish and arriving at the all-important harvest maximum

511
00:40:19,521 --> 00:40:22,920
is a major undertaking that requires human observers,

512
00:40:24,040 --> 00:40:26,680
acoustic science, and a little fishing.

513
00:40:27,800 --> 00:40:31,980
The Net Shed at the Alaska Fisheries Science Center in Seattle

514
00:40:31,981 --> 00:40:36,220
builds and repairs massive trawl nets for scientists turned fishermen.

515
00:40:36,940 --> 00:40:39,420
We use these nets for stock assessment purposes.

516
00:40:39,780 --> 00:40:43,200
We go out, we catch fish, we make the same toes year after year,

517
00:40:43,201 --> 00:40:47,120
and then we get a feel for where the stocks are going up, they're going down.

518
00:40:47,440 --> 00:40:53,640
We take samples, we look at the biology, we take link frequencies, we take sets of the fish,

519
00:40:54,040 --> 00:40:58,520
and then you get a feel for what the aggregate of the fish are and the health of the stock in that area.

520
00:40:59,700 --> 00:41:03,120
One highly specialized fishing vessel is the Miller Freeman,

521
00:41:04,320 --> 00:41:08,140
a floating research laboratory capable of finding and counting fish

522
00:41:08,141 --> 00:41:11,620
in a 5 million square kilometer area of the Bering Sea.

523
00:41:13,200 --> 00:41:17,300
We run a uniformly spaced parallel transect pattern.

524
00:41:17,400 --> 00:41:19,060
It's basically like going out and mowing the yard,

525
00:41:19,500 --> 00:41:24,120
but collecting acoustic data and information from net catches along those parallel transects.

526
00:41:25,380 --> 00:41:28,040
And what we do is we use a scientific echo sounder,

527
00:41:28,580 --> 00:41:33,680
as well as trawls, large nets, either mid-water nets or bottom nets,

528
00:41:33,840 --> 00:41:36,800
to sample the acoustic targets that we encounter.

529
00:41:37,960 --> 00:41:42,820
The researchers chase large schools of pollock to sample population density.

530
00:41:43,200 --> 00:41:45,560
and the ratio of juveniles to adults.

531
00:41:47,900 --> 00:41:52,040
The final part of the equation is the NOAA observer program.

532
00:41:53,200 --> 00:41:54,026
As in most U.S.

533
00:41:54,027 --> 00:41:57,880
fisheries, trained observers are placed aboard many commercial vessels

534
00:41:57,881 --> 00:42:05,380
to get a first-hand view of the intended catch and the unintended bycatch.

535
00:42:06,000 --> 00:42:10,580
Well, their principal responsibility is to collect information on catch quantity and composition,

536
00:42:10,581 --> 00:42:13,460
to document that and then to send it forward.

537
00:42:13,880 --> 00:42:16,860
There are bycatch issues that really affect fishing opportunities,

538
00:42:17,080 --> 00:42:19,580
and in some cases fisheries can be closed or curtailed

539
00:42:19,581 --> 00:42:22,040
because of bycatch of salmon or halibut.

540
00:42:24,440 --> 00:42:28,200
Observers transmit a near real-time report on catch composition

541
00:42:28,201 --> 00:42:30,540
and any bycatch issues.

542
00:42:31,540 --> 00:42:35,300
This information is reviewed by commercial fleet managers in Seattle,

543
00:42:35,620 --> 00:42:40,380
who can then immediately redirect the fishing to an area without the bycatch problems.

544
00:42:40,580 --> 00:42:47,580
This relationship between fishermen and scientists is critical to the health of America's largest fishery.

545
00:42:47,581 --> 00:42:50,580
But the Alaskan waters are unique.

546
00:42:50,581 --> 00:42:56,580
Today, nine of the world's 17 major fisheries are considered overfished or threatened.

547
00:42:56,581 --> 00:43:01,580
Rather than saving them, better technology may be leading to their demise.

548
00:43:01,581 --> 00:43:05,580
I think since World War II, that technology has increased so much.

549
00:43:05,581 --> 00:43:10,020
sonar, depth finders, got greater speed, you know, just so many things that made

550
00:43:10,021 --> 00:43:16,920
fishing more effective. Efficiency is devastating to a fishery. What

551
00:43:16,921 --> 00:43:25,260
efficiency means is that you produce more with fewer people. Ideally, a

552
00:43:25,261 --> 00:43:29,320
fishery should be doing just the opposite. They should be catching less fish and

553
00:43:29,321 --> 00:43:35,100
employing more fishermen. But the Bering Sea may predict a different future.

554
00:43:35,101 --> 00:43:41,120
One that combines efficiency and science to harvest yet safeguard the ocean's bounty.

555
00:43:42,120 --> 00:43:47,040
For every 15 pollock we catch, there's still 85 of them swimming in the Bering Sea,

556
00:43:47,041 --> 00:43:50,620
even though we catch over 1.3 million metric tons.

557
00:43:50,920 --> 00:43:53,860
So when you have a golden goose like the North Pacific,

558
00:43:54,100 --> 00:43:58,060
it really is not in the best interest of the industry to kill the thing off.

559
00:43:59,680 --> 00:44:03,500
In some U.S. fisheries, conservation is a major priority.

560
00:44:05,100 --> 00:44:10,000
Aquaculture and better fishery science will hopefully take pressure off wild stocks.

561
00:44:11,000 --> 00:44:16,560
But without proper management, the future for all fisheries may be an empty net.

562
00:44:17,840 --> 00:44:21,080
Meanwhile, a determined group of men keep fishing,

563
00:44:22,500 --> 00:44:28,960
struggling to maintain a proud tradition in nature's threatening and threatened seas.

